<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dekulakization</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1929">1929</a>–<a href="/years/1933">1933</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Raskulachivaniye</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dekulakization was a campaign of repression in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> against the so-called kulaks, a loosely defined category of supposedly wealthy or exploitative peasants, begun after Stalin announced the liquidation of the kulaks as a class on 27 December 1929. It aimed to break resistance to collectivization and to supply forced labour for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, the Urals, Kazakhstan and the North. Between 2.1 and 2.3 million people were deported to special settlements in 1930–1933 and 20,000 to 30,000 executed by troiki; Manfred Hildermeier estimates 530,000 to 600,000 deaths, excluding the famine. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportations</a> became a cornerstone of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a> and helped bring about the famine of 1930–1933.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dekulakization.jpg" alt="Dekulakization" width="330" height="275" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown. Thanks to Lewis H. Siegelbaum and Andrej K. Sokolov · GFDL · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dekulakisation_in_the_USSR_V_3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
